     8	## What the V1 exit establishes
     9	
    10	**The claim.** A person holds a V1 receipt, an authenticated verifier release and its trust file. Without
    11	Tessera, that person can check four things. The receipt binds exact bytes. Both service keys signed frames that bind
    12	those bytes. A DNSSEC record and a signed git commit named those keys for `wamason.com`. The bytes and both
    13	signatures existed before a specific Bitcoin block. Every item depends on the registered assumptions about
    14	cryptography, key use, Bitcoin history and the verifier release.
    15	
    16	**The adversary.** The attacker controls the network and can change, strip or move any part of a receipt. The
    17	verifier rejects those changes. The attacker can use its own keys. If the authority records are not captured, the
    18	verifier rejects those receipts. If the attacker captures **both** the DNS record and the git repository, its new
    19	receipts under its own keys **pass**. Receipts that the honest service signed stay protected, while the service keys
    20	stay secret and unmisused. An attacker who captures the issuing AWS account can make receipts that pass.
    21	
    22	**The boundaries.** V1 does not check who sent the bytes, what they mean, revocation, or past key or algorithm
    23	safety. It gives no lower time bound. T is a Bitcoin header time, not real time. A separate real-time bound U holds
    24	only under A-UTC, which the verifier does not check. V1 detects no capture and checks no publication. The same
    25	content can have more than one valid receipt.
    26	
    27	**What the exit is.** The models, proofs, vectors and pages of A9.2 exist and passed their registered checks. With
    28	your signed opening commit, the exit permits V1-profile code only. It discharges nothing in the original Band 0 gate.
