   375	## A10.3 Reorganization depth, at k = 6 and `DepthK = k − 1` — OWNER DECISION, NARROW-CLAIM (review A03)
   376	
   377	A9 excludes "Bitcoin history deeper than k" (:49-50) and calls "Reorganization deeper than k" a residual (C5,
   378	:144). A9 also imports k = 6 under `DepthK = k − 1` (:105) and A2.1's count, in which the including block is the
   379	first confirmation.
   380	
   381	**Definition.** The depth of a reorganization is the number of blocks it removes from the tip of the chain. A
   382	reorganization of depth d removes every block that has d or fewer confirmations when it occurs. At k = 6
   383	confirmations, five blocks follow the anchor block; P5c's `depth` counts those five (`depth = c − 1`, so
   384	`DepthK = 5`).
   385	
   386	**Correction.** The registered assumption is that anchors with k or more confirmations are permanent (A1.6:
   387	"anchors at depth ≥ k are treated as permanent"). P5c's `Reorg` action can orphan an anchor only while
   388	`depth < DepthK`, that is, at five or fewer confirmations. So a reorganization of depth exactly k, which removes an
   389	anchor with k confirmations, is outside both. A9's "deeper than k" read literally leaves that case inside the
   390	claim, with no evidence. The excluded residual is therefore **a reorganization of depth k or more**. This narrows
   391	A9's literal prose claim at equality. It changes neither the model nor its permanence assumption, and withdraws no
   392	checked model result. This does not affect C4's chain-order gap (`g + 5 < r`), which the review found correct. The
   393	changed sentences are rows 13–14 of A10.7.
