     9	
    10	## 1. What a client gets
    11	
    12	The client sends bytes. Tessera returns a **receipt**: a bundle that anyone can check later, without Tessera
    13	running and without our help.
    14	
    15	## 2. Guarantees
    16	
    17	**Conditions.** Every guarantee assumes the registered cryptographic, key-use, authority, chain-history and
    18	distribution assumptions (A9:32–52, A9:128–136). In particular: nobody misused the service's signing capability,
    19	SHA-256 and OpenTimestamps hold, and the checker's verifier release is authentic.
    20	
    21	When those hold, a checker who has the receipt, the published verifier and its trust configuration can establish:
    22	
    23	| # | Guarantee | Source |
    24	|---|---|---|
    25	| G1 | **Exact bytes.** The receipt binds these exact bytes. Change one bit and the check fails. | C1 |
    26	| G2 | **Presented to Tessera.** The bytes were presented to the Tessera V1 service at some issuance. | C2 |
    27	| G3 | **Both signatures.** Both of the service's keys signed. A missing, stripped or swapped signature fails. | C3 |
    28	| G4 | **The keys were Tessera's.** DNSSEC (`_tessera-v1.wamason.com`, at the snapshot's own anchored time) and a signed git commit (at its own evidence time) named the keys for the service's domain. The git commit was in the Bitcoin chain at least 6 blocks before the receipt. | C4, A10.6 |
    29	| G5 | **Existed by a block.** The signed bytes and both signatures existed before a specific Bitcoin block: the lowest-height qualifying block in the presented proof, confirmed 6 deep. | C5, A10.1, A10.5 |
    30	| G6 | **A real-time bound, under an unchecked assumption.** A-UTC says at least one of that block and its 5 successors has a time stamp at most 2 hours behind when it was made. If A-UTC holds, the bytes existed before U (the latest of those 6 times, plus 2 hours). U is reported separately and never changes the verdict. | A10.1.1 |
    31	| G7 | **Window, two checks only.** The declared issue time is not before the keys' window opened, and the chain time T is not after it closed. This does not prove the signing happened inside the window. | C4, A10.2 |
    32	| G8 | **No Tessera needed.** The verdict depends only on the inputs above. | C6 |
    33	| G9 | **A typed verdict.** Once the trust configuration is authenticated, the verifier returns `VALID_STRICT`, `INVALID` or `UNVERIFIABLE`, with a reason code and `revocation: NOT_EVALUATED`, `historical_security: NOT_EVALUATED`, `standing: ABSENT/NOT_ISSUED_IN_PROFILE`. Missing required evidence prevents acceptance. A valid receipt still leaves everything in §3 unestablished. If the trust configuration is not authenticated, there is no receipt verdict. | C7; A9.5 trust-configuration authority; V1-PROFILE §5 (`TRUST_CONFIG_UNAUTHENTICATED`) |
    34	| G10 | **Honest receipts cannot be moved.** If an attacker takes over the DNS and git authority records while the service's own keys stay secret and unmisused, it cannot move honestly signed frames to other keys or bytes, or change their signer sets, whether they were signed before or after the takeover. | A10.4 |
    35	**No G11.** The service-key listing (A10.4.1) was cut from V1 on 2026-10-19 under A10 row 33
    36	(`docs/decisions/2026-10-19-listing-cut.md`). G10 holds while the independent release boundary of A9.5 holds.
    37	**New receipts under an attacker's own keys pass when both authority channels are captured.**
    38	
    39	## 3. Not guaranteed
    40	
    41	Each of these is a deliberate limit, not a missing feature.
    42	
    43	- **Who submitted the bytes**, or that a replayed receipt suits the current transaction (case f).
    44	- **What the bytes mean**, or that they are true. A contract inside the bytes is the checker's to verify (cases i, i′).
    45	- **That a key was not revoked** (cases e, e′).
    46	- **That an algorithm or a key was still safe** when it signed.
    47	- **Any lower time bound or priority.** "Existed by" is never "existed after" or "came first".
    48	- **Real time without A-UTC.** Without it, only chain order is claimed.
    49	- **Standing** of any kind.
    50	- **Separate control of the two keys.** One controller holds both. Capturing the issuing AWS account means both
    51	  slots can sign.
    52	- **Visibility.** Publishing the authority records is the issuer's duty; the verifier does not check it. V1
    53	  guarantees no detection or public exposure of a capture, of new receipts, or of a release given to one party.
    54	- **That DNSSEC shows publication before issue.**
    55	- **Anything after a Bitcoin reorganization of 6 or more blocks.**
    56	- **Renewal, degraded modes, or wrappers.** These are refused (`UNVERIFIABLE`), including honest ones (cases d2, h:
    57	  the two registered false rejects).
    58	- **One receipt per presentation.** A receipt id (SHA-256 of `A_sig`) names a receipt, not a presentation. Signing
    59	  is randomized, so the same honest frames can carry more than one valid signature set and therefore more than one
    60	  valid receipt, each with its own T. Each still meets G1–G10. (Owner, 2026-10-19, from finding E-02 of the first
    61	  real run.)
    62	- **Stability of T and U across different proofs.** A different proof or pin set can select a different block.
    63	- **Protection for a checker who skips authenticating the verifier release** or runs a substituted one.
    64	
    65	## 4. Failure model
    66	
    67	| Failure or attacker | What still holds |
    68	|---|---|
    69	| Tessera disappears | Everything in §2, if the holder kept the receipt and the verifier release. Reconstruction after the project dies is not demonstrated. |
    70	| Attacker alters a receipt or moves its signatures | Rejected (G1, G3, G10). |
    71	| Attacker uses its own keys, authority records not captured | Rejected: the required authority evidence does not name them (G4). |
    72	| Attacker captures both DNS and git authority records | Honest receipts protected (G10). New receipts under its own keys **pass** (the listing was cut). |
    73	| Attacker captures the issuing AWS account | **Not protected.** It can issue receipts that verify. |
    74	| Attacker steals the release key | Not protected: it can publish releases with a trust configuration of its choosing. This is distribution compromise, which A9 excludes (A9:47–52). A checker who installs no later release is unaffected. |
    75	| Bitcoin miners lie about time | G5 holds (chain order). G6 holds only under A-UTC. |
    76	| Bitcoin reorganizes fewer than 6 blocks | G5 holds. |
    77	
    78	## 5. Pairing: where each guarantee is checked
    79	
    80	The method needs every guarantee checked twice: once by a formal model or inspection, once by tests against the
    81	implementation. When the two disagree, we fix the contract, the model or the code. This is a first mapping, not
    82	yet checked case by case.
    83	
    84	**These are mappings, not pairs.** A pair, in the method's sense, runs the same scenario on both sides, so that a
    85	disagreement means something. Here the model side and the test side were written separately, for different
    86	scenarios, and none of the rows below runs one scenario through both. The tupay project's M1 found the same gap in
    87	its own workflow ("the pairs were not pairs", `../tupay/experiments/M1-result.md`). Making real pairs (shared
    88	scenarios, and a `derives: G…` tag on each case and model element) is post-freeze work. (Owner, 2026-10-21.)
    89	
    90	| # | Model side | Test side (`prototype/conformance/cases-index.json` groups) |
    91	|---|---|---|
    92	| G1 | P1, P8 (prose proof and vectors) | container, codec, field grammar, numbers, spec binding, receipt consistency, OTS |
    93	| G2 | P1 | A9.1 mapping |
    94	| G3 | P2, P3 | signature profile, possession, hybrid semantics, hybrid routing, hybrid positive, keys, receipt consistency |
    95	| G4 | P10, S-P3: abstract authority and key binding only | DNSSEC, DNS record, git, chain order, authority isolation, pinned D |
    96	| G5 | P5, P5c: temporal and confirmation rules. Existence before the block is an assumption, not modelled. | OTS, proofs, time |
    97	| G6 | none | UTC assessment (UTC-01..UTC-10: honest, nonmonotone ×2, 2106 boundary, lowest-height segment, reported with INVALID and UNVERIFIABLE, null with T null ×3); `prototype/indep-verifier/tests/test_utc_assessment.py` (11 tests, including the real-1 receipt's headers 972187..972192); `prototype/e2e/tests/test_driver_dry.py::test_dry_run_utc_assessment`. Built 2026-10-19 (profile §7.4). A-UTC itself is unchecked by design |
    98	| G7 | none (P5/P5c model related rules, not these endpoints) | time |
    99	| G8 | P9: contract and code inspection; no discharging model is required | offline reproducibility vectors not built (`release checker` tests configuration updates) |
   100	| G9 | P4 | dispatch, aggregation, A9.1 mapping, container (d2), trust config |
   101	| G10 | S-P3 (honest-material key binding) | none named; honest-material transplant tests to be identified or built |
   102	
   103	**Gaps this table shows.**
   104	- Model missing: G6 (tests added 2026-10-19; A-UTC is an assumption, not a modelled property), G7's endpoints, and
   105	  concrete authority (G4 beyond the abstract model).
   106	- Tests missing: G8's offline reproducibility vectors, G10's honest-material transplant cases.
   107	
   108	## 6. What the tester does (4 December)
   109	
   110	One independent tester, who meets the criteria in `docs/V1-TESTER-BRIEF.md`, takes a receipt bundle and the
   111	verifier release and runs the verifier. For each case in A9.1's scenario mapping, the tester decides from the
   112	documentation alone whether to rely on the receipt, with no help from the project. **Pass:** no unsafe accepts,
   113	and false rejects only on d2 and h. The result is that one tester's result.
     1	# The service-key listing is cut from V1 (A10 row 33)
     2	
     3	> **OWNER DECISION, 2026-10-19** (Claude Opus 5.5, owner instance). GATE action that the registered text already
     4	> provides: A10 row 33 makes the listing the second item cut and states what applies when it is cut. No amendment.
     5	> The author adopted option (e) on 2026-10-18, including row 33. He can override this cut until the freeze assembly
     6	> (26 Oct, `docs/V1-EXIT-30OCT.md`); after that, putting the listing back means a new spec object.
     7	
     8	> **CORRECTION, 2026-10-21 (owner).** This cut is **not** authorized by the registered text as claimed below. A10 row 33
     9	> amends A9.2's cut order, and that order applies only "if the 30 Oct exit slips" (A9.2). The exit had not slipped;
    10	> the cut rested on a forecast of about one day of slack. It reverses part of option (e), which the author adopted
    11	> on 2026-10-18, so it is the **author's ruling**, put to him in `docs/E9-READ-PACKET.md`. Until he rules, the freeze
    12	> is assembled without the listing and nothing is final before the opening commit. The owner still recommends the
    13	> cut, on the merits in "Why" items 2 and 3; item 1 (schedule) is weaker since the freeze was assembled on
    14	> 2026-10-21, not 2026-10-26. The original text is left below unchanged.
    15	
    16	## What is cut
    17	
    18	A10.4.1 and the rows that take effect only under option (e) with it: 25–32 and 37–39. These are the `service_keys`
    19	trust-configuration member, its classifier and reason codes, the listing pre-commitment, the C0/C1 bootstrap, and
    20	the listing-evidence package that relying parties would have to keep outside the bundle.
    21	
    22	## What applies instead (row 33)
    23	
    24	A10.4 option (a): honest signed material stays protected under authority capture (rows 15–16; contract G10). **V1
    25	gives no protection against new receipts under a captor's own keys** when both authority channels are captured, and
    26	the relying-party text and the freeze record say so. Release-key custody (A10.4.2) is unchanged: the cut does not
    27	cure a failure of release-key independence, and the custody gate at the first public verifier release still holds.
    28	
    29	## Why
    30	
    31	1. **Schedule.** The 30 Oct exit audit (`docs/V1-EXIT-30OCT.md`) puts the listing on the critical path, with about
    32	   one working day of slack. Cutting it saves about 4 days (owner estimate). The first cut item, the PQ hybrid, is
    33	   already registered and would save almost nothing.
    34	2. **What it buys is narrow.** It protects only relying parties whose verifier release is authentic. After review
    35	   r4 its protection against a later release-key theft needs independently authenticated Bitcoin history. It moves
    36	   trust from two credentials (DNS and repository key) to a third (the release key), and all three have the same
    37	   custodian.
    38	3. **What it costs relying parties.** A listing-evidence package outside the receipt bundle that holders must keep
    39	   (contract review VC-02). This is the one mechanism added purely for a decades-later adjudicator
    40	   (`docs/decisions/2026-10-19-horizon-cost.md`), and it is the one the author asked about and got no clear answer to.
    41	
    42	## What is lost, honestly
    43	
    44	- Production receipts issued before any later listing can never have its protection. A listing added in a later
    45	  version protects only receipts after it.
    46	- The horizon analysis recommended keeping the listing at its signed scope, on that ground. The owner weighs the
    47	  schedule and the relying-party burden above it for V1.
    48	
    49	## Registered as a candidate, not an obligation
    50	
    51	A key listing with a chain-order pre-commitment, or a witnessed log of key assertions, is a candidate for a later
    52	version. Neither creates an obligation.
     1	# Tessera plain-language style (inspired by ASD-STE100)
     2	
     3	Owner instance, 2026-10-16. The author asked for documentation "written so someone whose native language isn't
     4	Claude can understand it". These rules adapt the main writing rules of ASD-STE100 (Simplified Technical English).
     5	We do not claim STE conformance: we have no approved STE dictionary check. We use the structure rules and our own
     6	dictionary.
     7	
     8	Revised 2026-10-16 after the faithfulness review of the A9 pilot (`docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md`,
     9	"Proposed dictionary work"). The revision adds the entries that the review lists and repairs receipt, anchor time,
    10	window, authority record, key, AWS key, VALID and INVALID. It replaces "Google key" with "second key". It removes
    11	"receipt ID", because its object was not defined. These entries are owner proposals. A reviewer must check each
    12	new entry against its registered source before publication.
    13	
    14	## Rules
    15	
    16	1. One sentence gives one idea.
    17	2. A sentence has a maximum of 20 words if it tells the reader to do something, and 25 words if it describes something.
    18	3. A paragraph has a maximum of 6 sentences and one topic. A fragment such as a list item counts as a sentence.
    19	4. Use the active voice. Say who or what does the action.
    20	5. Use only the words in the dictionary below for project ideas. Each word has one meaning. Do not use a synonym.
    21	6. Use technical names (for example "DNSSEC", "Bitcoin", "SHA-256") without change. Explain each one when you first use it.
    22	7. Do not use idioms, metaphors or humour.
    23	8. Write steps and conditions as numbered or bulleted lists.
    24	9. Write "must" for a rule and "can" for a possibility. Do not use "should", "may" or "shall".
    25	10. Do not refer to a document section number in the body. Put references at the end.
    26	
    27	## Faithfulness
    28	
    29	A plain document does not replace the signed or governing text. If the two differ, the governing text applies, and
    30	the difference is a finding. Before publication, a reviewer checks each plain document against its source. The
    31	reviewer confirms three things: the plain document contains no new rule, it removes no rule, and it changes no
    32	meaning. The reviewer records each place where the source was unclear or contradictory.
    33	
    34	A plain document does not resolve an unclear source. It renders the source as written and adds a visible marker,
    35	for example "[A9 is unclear here: see A10 draft, item A01.]". The marker names the item that must resolve it.
    36	
    37	## Dictionary
    38	
    39	Code-style names (for example `A_sig`, `NOT_EVALUATED`) are technical identifiers. Use them without change and
    40	explain them at first use.
    41	
    42	### Content, receipts and the service
    43	
    44	| Word | Meaning | Do not use |
    45	|---|---|---|
    46	| content | The bytes that a customer gives to the service. | data, document, payload |
    47	| receipt | The set of files that the service gives back for one content. A receipt is the evidence. | attestation (alone), certificate, bundle |
    48	| service | The Tessera V1 program that makes receipts. | Tessera (alone), issuer |
    49	| issuance event | One occasion on which the service makes a receipt for content. | issuance (alone) |
    50	| issue time | The time that the service writes into the frames when it signs. The verifier cannot check this time directly. | declared time |
    51	| holder | The person who keeps a receipt. | custodian |
    52	| user | The person who uses a verifier to make a decision about a receipt. | relying party (in plain text) |
    53	| attacker | A person who tries to make a false receipt, to change a true receipt, or to make a false claim about a receipt. | adversary |
    54	| operational sphere | Everything that the author uses to operate the service: the accounts, devices, credentials and signing capability. | — |
    55	| registered | Written in the signed amendments to the Tessera pre-registration. | — |
    56	
    57	### Keys and signatures
    58	
    59	| Word | Meaning | Do not use |
    60	|---|---|---|
    61	| key | A private signing key of the service. V1 has two keys, one in each slot. | signer |
    62	| slot | The named position of one service key in a receipt. V1 has two slots, `kms` and `gpg`. | — |
    63	| AWS key | The service key in the `kms` slot. It is in a hardware security module in the service's AWS account. The registered branch sets its signature algorithm. | kms key |
    64	| second key | The service key in the `gpg` slot. It is outside the AWS account, in non-exportable hardware. The dated custody record names that hardware. It uses ECDSA P-256. | Google key, gpg key, token |
    65	| key fingerprint | A short value that the verifier computes from a public key. It identifies that key. | key ID |
    66	| repository key | The key that signs the git commits that hold the git authority record. It is not a service key. | — |
    67	| DNS root key | A key of the DNS root zone that the trust file lists. DNSSEC checks start from it. | KSK (in plain text) |
    68	| release key | The key that authenticates each verifier release and its trust file. | — |
    69	| signature | The result when a key signs bytes. | — |
    70	| signature algorithm | The method that a key uses to make signatures and that a verifier uses to check them. | cipher |
    71	| signing capability | The ability to make a key sign. A person can have this ability without a copy of the private key. | key possession |
    72	| non-exportable | A property of a key in hardware: nobody can copy the private key out of the hardware. It does not stop use of the key. | — |
    73	| authority-record credentials | The credentials that let a person change an authority record: the DNS credential and the repository key. | channel credentials |
    74	
    75	### What the keys sign
    76	
    77	| Word | Meaning | Do not use |
    78	|---|---|---|
    79	| bind | Bytes bind a value when they fix that exact value, directly or through its SHA-256 hash. | — |
    80	| frame | The exact bytes that one service key signs. Each frame binds the content and the common metadata, and names the key fingerprint for its slot. | signed part, payload |
    81	| common metadata | The fields that both frames must contain with the same values. They include the domain name of the service. | — |
    82	| `A_sig` | The bytes that hold both frames and their exact signatures in a fixed format. | signed core |
    83	| receipt anchor | The SHA-256 hash of `A_sig`. OpenTimestamps puts this hash into Bitcoin. | — |
    84	
    85	### Authority
    86	
    87	| Word | Meaning | Do not use |
    88	|---|---|---|
    89	| authority record | A record that says which two keys belong to wamason.com and states their window. V1 has two authority records: one in DNS and one in a git commit. | key publication, channel |
    90	| window | The period that the authority records state for the two keys. It has a start and an end. The verifier compares the issue time with the start and T with the end. It makes no other comparison with the window. | lifetime, validity |
    91	| evidence time | The time at which the verifier checks one authority record. It is the chain time of that record's own selected block. | — |
    92	| DNS snapshot | A saved copy of the DNS records and DNSSEC signatures for the DNS authority record. The service anchors it in Bitcoin. | — |
    93	| DNS owner name | The complete DNS name that holds a DNS record. | — |
    94	| commit | A git object that records a version of the files in a git repository. A key can sign a commit. | — |
    95	| tree | A git object that lists the files and folders of one version. | — |
    96	| blob | A git object that holds the bytes of one file. | — |
    97	| object ID | The SHA-1 hash that git uses to identify a git object. | commit hash |
    98	
    99	### Bitcoin and time
   100	
   101	| Word | Meaning | Do not use |
   102	|---|---|---|
   103	| Bitcoin block | One block of the Bitcoin chain. Each block holds hashes and a header. | — |
   104	| block number | The position of a block in the Bitcoin chain. The first block has number 0. | height (in plain text) |
   105	| header | The part of a Bitcoin block that identifies the block and states its time. | — |
   106	| chain time | The time that a block header states. It is a value from Bitcoin, not a measurement of real time. | block time |
   107	| confirmation | The block that contains an anchor gives the first confirmation. Each later block gives one more confirmation. | — |
   108	| reorganization | A change in the Bitcoin chain that removes recent blocks and puts other blocks in their place. | reorg |
   109	| OpenTimestamps proof | A file that connects a hash to one or more Bitcoin blocks. | OTS proof (in plain text), timestamp |
   110	| Bitcoin attestation | A statement in an OpenTimestamps proof that a Bitcoin block at a stated block number holds a hash. It is not a receipt. | attestation (alone) |
   111	| proof path | The steps in an OpenTimestamps proof from the hash to one Bitcoin attestation. | branch |
   112	| selected block | The block of the Bitcoin attestation that the verifier selects from an OpenTimestamps proof. | — |
   113	| anchor time (T) | The chain time of the selected block for the receipt anchor. T is a chain time, not real time. | timestamp |
   114	| pin | An entry in the trust file that gives the exact header for one block number. | checkpoint |
   115	
   116	### The verifier and its results
   117	
   118	| Word | Meaning | Do not use |
   119	|---|---|---|
   120	| verifier | The program that a person uses to check a receipt. | validator |
   121	| trust file | The file that comes with a verifier. It lists the DNS root keys, the repository key and the pins. | trust configuration |
   122	| verdict | The result of a check: VALID, INVALID or CANNOT VERIFY. | status, outcome |
   123	| VALID | All of V1's registered checks passed. This does not mean that V1 checked revocation, historical security, standing, the sender or the truth of the content. | VALID_STRICT (in plain text) |
   124	| INVALID | A registered check failed. INVALID does not show that the content is false. | — |
   125	| CANNOT VERIFY | The verifier does not have what it needs to decide. This is not the same as INVALID. | UNVERIFIABLE (in plain text) |
   126	| cryptographic assumption | A registered condition that V1 does not check: the hash functions and signature algorithms are not broken. | — |
   127	| key-use assumption | A registered condition that V1 does not check: each key signs only the bytes that its rules permit. | key rules |
   128	| Bitcoin history assumption | A registered condition that V1 does not check: the Bitcoin chain history stays intact and available. | — |
   129	| revocation | A statement that a key must no longer be trusted. | — |
   130	| historical security | Whether a signature algorithm or key was still safe at the time when it signed. | historical safety |
   131	| status capture | Evidence about the revocation status of a key, saved after the anchor. V1 does not require it. | — |
   132	| standing | Evidence of which result the service gave to an attempt: the shipped receipt, or an attempt that it replaced, refused or did not ship. | — |
   133	| renewal | The addition of later evidence to a receipt, so that the receipt stays verifiable after an old mechanism becomes unsafe. | — |
   134	| wrapper | An outer layer of evidence around a receipt, with its own verification. | — |
   135	| degraded verification | Verification in which a policy waives some registered checks. | — |
   136	
   137	### The 4 December test and build evidence
   138	
   139	| Word | Meaning | Do not use |
   140	|---|---|---|
   141	| relying-party test | The registered test on 4 December 2026. One independent person uses only V1's documentation to decide each case of the registered mapping. | usability test |
   142	| registered mapping | The registered list of test cases, with the expected verdict and the safe decision for each case. | scenario set |
   143	| unsafe accept | A decision to rely on a claim that the evidence does not establish. It can concern a VALID receipt. | bad accept |
   144	| false reject | A decision to reject an honest receipt. | — |
   145	| independence conditions | The registered conditions for the test person: a computing background, no knowledge of this field or project, no talk with the author about Tessera, and no help during the test. | — |
   146	| negative control | A test that must fail for a program with a known defect. It shows that the test can find that defect. | — |
   147	| companion verifier | A verifier with one deliberate defect, made for a negative control. | broken verifier |

