     1	# Project Tessera
     2	
     3	An AWS-native attestation service, built as a public demonstration of
     4	principled engineering with AI as collaborator rather than substitute.
     5	
     6	The primary objective is not the product alone. It is evidence that a
     7	senior engineer can close a real knowledge gap quickly—using AI for
     8	mechanics while owning architecture, judgment, and every decision that
     9	must be defended cold. The secondary objective is commercial viability
    10	as attestation-as-a-service; that upside is measured separately and is
    11	not allowed to expand the demonstration’s scope.
    12	
    13	**Credibility line** (as registered, `docs/phase-0-prereg.md` §1):
    14	every architecture decision here is the human author (Tony) in
    15	collaboration with AI agents. The purpose of the AI agents is to
    16	accelerate the typing, push back on my decisions, clarify them, but not
    17	to _make_ those decisions without consultation.
    18	
    19	Since 17 September 2026 an AI instance owns the project day to day, by
    20	the author's delegation; decisions it makes are labelled OWNER DECISION
    21	in the record, and the author's are labelled as his. An earlier wording
    22	of this paragraph, which was not the registered text, was read as
    23	requiring the author to reconstruct every decision unaided. The author
    24	never intended that requirement, and it is not one.
    25	
    26	Tony Mason  
    27	Initiated 10 June 2026  
    28	This README reflects the repository as of 12 September 2026 (Band 0 — the formal foundation, H0). Status refreshed by the AI collaborator under the July delegation; the credibility line and reading order are the author's.
    29	
    30	---
    31	
    32	## What this repository is
    33	
    34	Tessera provides a neutral third party’s cryptographic binding to a
    35	package of information at a fixed point in time. Attestation here means:
    36	*this is what was presented in a bundle*, tied to a temporal anchor that
    37	cannot be trivially regenerated. It does not by itself vouch for the
    38	authenticity or truth of the contents; those guarantees, if any, ride in
    39	the package.
    40	
    41	The service is a cousin of [Willay](https://github.com/fsgeek/willay):
    42	shared cryptographic and modeling ethos, forked edges for cloud-native
    43	operation (KMS, S3 Object Lock, queues, observability, IaC).
    44	
    45	The method is as load-bearing as the artifact:
    46	
    47	1. **Pre-registration** — objectives, hypotheses, properties, and success
    48	   criteria are written, signed, and OpenTimestamps-anchored *before*
    49	   outcomes are known. The journal is measured against that record,
    50	   including honest divergence.
    51	2. **Formal foundation first (Band 0 / H0)** — machine-checked models of
    52	   the core guarantees gate later code. No Phase 1 until the foundation
    53	   holds or is amended on the record.
    54	3. **Public, falsifiable process** — multi-round adversarial review,
    55	   broken companions that must go red, named residuals, and a living
    56	   property tracker. Theater is a failure mode, not a feature.
    57	
    58	---
    59	
    60	## Current status (12 September 2026)
    61	
    62	| Layer | Status |
    63	|-------|--------|
    64	| Phase 0 pre-registration | Signed and amended (Amendments 1–6 in force; A3 adopted 2026-08-08/09 after a four-model cold panel; A4 signed 2026-09-06 at `5188e7a`, precision repairs and discharge paths; A5 signed 2026-09-12 at `504e662`, the probe and cross-family findings: three verifier obligations, the waived-evidence reading, the §A4.6 boundary; A6 adopted 2026-09-12, P7's discharge path, signed at the commit carrying it; the 2026-08-29 author clarification records that "before any code" means code intended for the service) |
    65	| Band 0 formal models | Active — TLA+ (P4, P5/P6, P5c issuance, bridge, floor-structure probe; non-discharging P9/P10 probes); ProVerif first-link spike complete, mechanism **decided** 2026-08-13 (`formal/spike/first-link/DECISION.md`); symbolic suite **built and run**: S-P3, S-P1, S-P2, S-P7, S-STANDING (`formal/suite/`), each cross-family reviewed by a non-author model, headers read by the author 2026-09-12; tracker: P1–P7 and P10 `checked` (P7 on Amendment 6), P8 and P9 open, standing row added `open` (Band 0 exit gated on it); capstone composition and P8 not begun |
    66	| Named residual | The author has recorded (2026-08-29) that the first-link decision rests on symbolic results he cannot yet defend cold; accepted, not waived, with closing conditions |
    67	| Cloud-independent crypto core | Not yet frozen (gated on Band 0) |
    68	| LocalStack / AWS vertical slice | Not started |
    69	| Application code | Minimal scaffolding only; one non-production design probe under `formal/spike/standing-probe/probe/` |
    70	
    71	We are deliberately early in product code and late in protocol precision.
    72	That ordering is intentional.
    73	
    74	---
    75	
    76	## How to read this repository
    77	
    78	Recommended order for a new reader (human or collaborator instance):
    79	
    80	1. **This file** — orientation and credibility line.
    81	2. [`docs/phase-0-prereg.md`](docs/phase-0-prereg.md) — the signed intent
    82	   and ranked objectives.
    83	3. [`docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md`](docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-1.md)
    84	   — property list, adversary model, temporal-anchor semantics, agreement
    85	   gate as falsification task.
    86	4. [`docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-2.md`](docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-2.md)
    87	   — refinement of confirmation, refusal, anchor standing, and the
    88	   clock-roles ruling (adopted 2026-07-21, three review rounds).
    89	5. [`docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-3.md`](docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-3.md)
    90	   — identity boundary, linked evidence floors, the symbolic suite and
    91	   capstone gate, survivability, standing as an orthogonal dimension
    92	   (adopted 2026-08-08/09 after a four-model cold panel).
    93	6. [`formal/spike/first-link/DECISION.md`](formal/spike/first-link/DECISION.md)
    94	   — the first mechanism decision made under the method: criteria
    95	   before evidence, provenance labels, the author's residual.
    96	7. [`docs/tessera-cloud-demo-spec.md`](docs/tessera-cloud-demo-spec.md) —
    97	   demonstration architecture and capability map.
    98	8. [`docs/travelog.md`](docs/travelog.md) — contemporaneous journal of
    99	   reality against the pre-registration.
   100	9. [`formal/PROPERTIES.md`](formal/PROPERTIES.md) and `formal/tla/` —
   101	   discharge status and the models themselves.
   102	10. [`docs/reviews/`](docs/reviews/) — adversarial review artifacts and
   103	    dispositions.
   104	
   105	If the tracker and an amendment disagree, the **amendment wins**.
   106	
   107	---
   108	
   109	## Disciplines this repository enforces
   110	
   111	- **GPG-signed commits** (author identity — *who*).
   112	- **OpenTimestamps anchors** on commits (existence in time — *when*).
   113	  These are two mechanisms; both are required for the public record.
   114	- **Amend, do not silently rewrite** historical pre-registration
   115	  documents.
   116	- **AI as collaborator for mechanics and pushback; author owns all
   117	  decisions** (division of labor is explicit in the pre-registration).
   118	- **Falsification-style review** before load-bearing text is signed
   119	  (see Amendment 1 §A1.7 and the review archive).
   120	
   121	Setup for local hooks and OTS tooling lives under `scripts/` (see
   122	`scripts/install-hooks.sh`).
   123	
   124	---
   125	
   126	## What this is not
   127	
   128	- Not a highlight reel. Pre-registration exists so success and failure
   129	  are both legible.
   130	- Not formal-methods theater. Models state what they prove, what they
   131	  assume, and what remains unclaimed (especially liveness vs. safety).
   132	- Not an unbounded product fantasy. Commercial upside is secondary and
   133	  deliberately constrained.
   134	
   135	---
   136	
   137	## License and contact
   138	
   139	See repository metadata and commit history for authorship. The public
   140	record of the work is the git history: signed, timestamped, and open to
   141	scrutiny.
   142	
   143	For the living narrative of how the work actually felt and where it
