     1	# E6a: the unknown-algorithm transition, with companions. TLC results, 2026-10-19
     2	
     3	> **STATUS: PROPOSED.** Produced by the AI collaborator (owner work, Claude Opus 5.5) on 2026-10-19. Not adopted;
     4	> the commit is the author's. This file covers exit item E6a (A9 §A9.2, "E6a with companions"; A3 §A3.6.2 item 3).
     5	> It changes no registered sentence. `formal/tla/P4_VerifierStates.tla` was **not edited**: sha256
     6	> `899ebd01…a609` before and after. It was re-run unchanged and reproduces its committed results (§5).
     7	> **Not done yet:** the time-boxed Codex falsification pass (`docs/V1-EXIT-30OCT.md` §11, 0.5 d) and the author's
     8	> E9 read. So this is `checked` (the tool passes), not `discharged`.
     9	
    10	## 1. What the models claim
    11	
    12	**Claim (plain language).** Take one presented input. A verifier that follows the rules gets these verdicts:
    13	
    14	- An algorithm id that is well-formed and correctly bound but unsupported gives `UNVERIFIABLE` (reason
    15	  `ALG_UNKNOWN`). This holds only when nothing else definitely failed. It is the only algorithm case that gives
    16	  `UNVERIFIABLE`.
    17	- These give `INVALID`: a missing id, an encoding mismatch, malformed parameters, an id prohibited by policy, a
    18	  substituted id, and a known algorithm whose signature fails.
    19	- An unknown algorithm never gives any `VALID` verdict, under any legal policy.
    20	- **Mixed case.** If any required check definitely fails, the verdict is `INVALID`, even when an unknown algorithm or
    21	  missing evidence is also present (A4 §A4.2).
    22	- **Missing signature versus missing evidence.** A missing or stripped required signature is a failure: `INVALID`,
    23	  whatever the algorithm (A9 C3; spec V28). A missing required evidence member cannot be checked: on its own it gives
    24	  `UNVERIFIABLE / MEMBER_MISSING`, never `VALID`, and never `INVALID` by itself.
    25	- **Dispatch.** An out-of-profile input gives `UNVERIFIABLE / OUT_OF_PROFILE`, and nothing else is evaluated (A9.5).
    26	  `OUT_OF_PROFILE` never arises after dispatch.
    27	- Every in-profile verdict is exactly P4's `VerdictFor`, applied to the per-check outcomes the input produces.
    28	
    29	**The adversary.** Here the adversary chooses the input. In one state, every slot has an algorithm form (7 forms), a
    30	signature form (absent, bad or good) and an evidence form (absent, bad or good). Dispatch is in or out, and the
    31	policy is any legal waiver set. TLC enumerates **every** combination: 7,938 states in the V1 instance and 15,876 in
    32	the general one. The broken companions are the other half. Each one is a verifier with one specific defect, and it
    33	must go red.
    34	
    35	**Boundaries. These are not claimed:**
    36	
    37	- *Instance.* The instance is fixed: two slots, two evidence members. V1 has no waivable check. The general instance
    38	  makes one evidence member (git) waivable.
    39	- *Codes.* The model has five reason classes. It does not choose among concrete `INVALID` codes, and it does not
    40	  model the total check order beyond one rule: row 2.2 `MEMBER_MISSING` comes before row 7.1 `ALG_UNKNOWN`.
    41	- *Classification.* Which bytes count as "the signature" and which as "evidence" is the profile's assignment. The
    42	  model takes the classes as given; see §6.
    43	- *Slots.* Waiving a whole signature slot (P2's degraded subset) is not modelled. Neither is cross-slot alg
    44	  equality (V1 row 6.2).
    45	- *Not covered.* Codecs, bytes, keys, time and the adversary's cryptographic actions belong to P1/P3/P7/P8 and the
    46	  symbolic suite.
    47	- *Instruments.* The conformance cases cited below are instrument material. They inform this mapping and discharge
    48	  nothing (A9 instrument rule).
    49	
    50	## 2. Files
    51	
    52	| File | Role |
    53	|---|---|
    54	| `E6a_Base.tla` | Inputs, `Init`, the check set, and `VerdictFrom` / `ReasonFrom`, which instantiate **P4's own `VerdictFor`** (parametrized `INSTANCE P4_VerifierStates`, found through `-DTLA-Library=formal/tla`). |
    55	| `E6a_Props.tla` | All 32 invariants and 13 vacuity witnesses, stated once over the raw inputs. Every module instantiates it, so the companions are checked against exactly the formulas the correct model is checked against. This avoids the P4 `_Broken` copy drift, where only 7 of 11 invariants were carried (2026-09-06 review item 5). |
    56	| `E6a_AlgVerdict.tla` | The correct model: the classification of inputs into check outcomes. |
    57	| `E6a_Broken_*.tla` (7) | The companions. Each differs from the correct module only on lines marked `BROKEN`. Each header records its predicted red set, written before any run. One prediction (DispatchFallthrough) was revised before its first run; the revision is noted in the file. |
    58	| `E6a_V1.cfg`, `E6a_General.cfg` | The main configurations, with all 32 invariants. |
    59	| `run.sh` | Regenerates everything in `runs/`. TLC 2026.07.31 (`~/.local/lib/tla2tools.jar`), `-workers 1`. |
    60	| `runs/SUMMARY.tsv` | One row per TLC run (495 runs). `runs/main`, `sanity`, `broken`, `matrix` (one log per companion and instance, 32 runs each) and `p4-rerun` hold the raw outputs. |
    61	
