   137	## 5. P4 kept intact
   138	
   139	`run.sh` re-runs P4's committed configurations, unchanged, into `runs/p4-rerun/`. Every result matches the committed
   140	`.out` files:
   141	
   142	| Run | Result |
   143	|---|---|
   144	| main | GREEN, 1,944 distinct |
   145	| Sanity (`-continue`) | all four witnesses violated, 1,944 violation reports, the same as committed |
   146	| `_Broken` | RED `StrictMeansEverything` |
   147	| `_Broken_Green` | RED `NoSilentPromotion` |
   148	| `_Broken_Green2` | GREEN |
   149	
   150	*Harness note:* the first `SUMMARY.tsv` row for P4 Sanity said GREEN. That was a classifier bug: under `-continue`,
   151	TLC also prints "No error has been found". The row and `run.sh` were corrected; the raw output was always correct.
   152	
   153	## 6. Observations for the owner (no change made)
   154	
   155	1. **What counts as a "missing signature" in V1.** The model follows A9 C3 ("a missing, stripped or substituted
   156	   one fails") and spec V28: a required signature that is absent is `INVALID`. Here is what V1 does with absent
   157	   signature material:
   158	   - A signature stripped *inside* a present `a_sig` fails in the codec: `SIG_EMPTY` / `TRUNCATED`, `INVALID`. This
   159	     matches the model.
   160	   - An absent `possession.<slot>` member is `UNVERIFIABLE / MEMBER_MISSING` (F03-j). So is a wholly absent `a_sig`
   161	     member (profile §2.1 and the §15.1 closed schema; no conformance case exists for this).
   162	
   163	   The profile therefore classes those two absences as *missing evidence*. Both outcomes are fail-closed, so nothing
   164	   is falsely accepted. But whether they are consistent with C3's "fails" is the owner's reading to record. The
   165	   model does not decide it.
   166	2. **Companion count.** The derivation asked for three companions: the fail-open companion, the mixed case, and
   167	   missing-signature versus missing-evidence. They are FailOpen, UnknownDominates/UnknownAsOutOfProfile, and the
   168	   MissingSig/MissingEvidence pair. The other two (MalformedAsUnknown, DispatchFallthrough) test the "exactly one
   169	   case" boundary and the dispatch gate. They were cheap and are recorded as extras.
