    69	## 1. The accepted domain, exactly
    70	
    71	Let **V** be the set of JSON values in E§1.1:
    72	- `null`, `true` and `false`;
    73	- finite binary64 numbers other than −0;
    74	- strings of Unicode scalar values that contain no noncharacter, no code point unassigned in Unicode 16.0.0, and are
    75	  in NFC;
    76	- arrays;
    77	- objects with pairwise distinct names.
    78	
    79	Two values are **equal** when they are equal as mathematical structures:
    80	- numbers are equal as doubles;
    81	- strings are equal as scalar sequences;
    82	- arrays are equal as sequences;
    83	- objects are equal as finite maps from names to values. Member order is not part of an object.
    84	
    85	That is the sense in which injectivity is claimed. Two JSON *texts* that differ only in spelling denote one value,
    86	and E§1.2 says why that is intended.
    87	
    88	The accepted sets are:
    89	
    90	- **Payloads.** For a tag `t`, `Pay(t) = { JCS(v) : v ∈ V, Schema_t(v), depth(v) ≤ 64, |JCS(v)| ≤ 2^24 }`. Here
    91	  `Schema_attest` is E§3.2 together with E§3.3, and `Schema_manifest` is E§4.
    92	- **Frames.** `Frm(t) = { frame(t, p) : p ∈ Pay(t) }` for `t ∈ {attest, manifest}`, where
   350	*Proof.* A valid trailer ends with `u32(6 + h)`, where `6 + h` is the length of its hashed header `04 00 pa ha ‖ u16
   351	h ‖ sub`. The whole trailer is that header, the two bytes `04 FF` and the four length bytes, so `|T| = 12 + h`:
   352	the final u32 plus six. Two decompositions `F ‖ T = F' ‖ T'` share their last four bytes, so `6 + h = 6 + h'` and
   353	`|T| = |T'|`. Both trailers are then the same-length suffix of one string, so `T = T'` and `F = F'`. (Corrected
   354	2026-10-19, P8F-05: the final u32 is the hashed-header length, not the trailer's own length; the split is the
   355	final u32 plus six.)
   356	
   357	Type 0x01 is excluded because text mode rewrites line-ending bytes, and those bytes occur in frame headers and
   358	lengths. ∎
   359	
   360	*What Lemma 9 does not say (NARROW-CLAIM, P8F-01).* Until 2026-10-19 this lemma also claimed that if one signature
   361	packet verifies for `F` and for `F'`, then under (H) `F' = F`. That does not follow: verification does not
   362	establish that the two hashes are equal. The falsification review built two distinct accepted attestation frames
   363	whose SHA-256 digests of `F ‖ T` differ (agreeing only in OpenPGP's left 16 bits) and one rule-O packet that
   364	verifies over both under one chosen P-256 key (`prototype/p8-falsify/lemma9-witness.json`). Whether one signature
   365	can verify over two different documents is a property of the signature scheme and of how the key was generated
   366	(P1, P3), not of the encoding. P8 does not claim it. Theorem (ii) uses only the split above; Theorem (iv) never
   367	used Lemma 9, and (iv)(c) already leaves verification to P1 and P3.
   368	
   369	**Remark 10 (no confusion with foreign formats).** Every V1 tagged string begins with a byte in 0x11–0x1d, which
   370	is below 0x20. Each of the following begins with a printable byte:
   371	- a git commit object (`tree …`, 0x74);
   372	- a TXT value of E§7 (`v=`, 0x76);
   373	- RFC 8785 text (`{`, `[`, `"`, a digit, `-`, `t`, `f` or `n`).
   374	
   375	So no V1 tagged string is byte-equal to any of them: a V1 signed document is never also a commit, a DNS value or
   376	RFC 8785 text. (K) keeps the slot keys off those formats. [OWNER 2026-10-19, P8F-01, NARROW-CLAIM] *(Was: "With (K)
   377	this is not needed, but it means that a key reused by mistake still could not have one of its V1 signatures read as
   378	a signature over a commit or a DNS value, or the reverse." Different documents do not by themselves prevent one
   379	signature from verifying over both; that is a signature-scheme property (Lemma 9's note), not P8's.)*
   380	
   381	**Lemma 11 (DNS record).** The unambiguity of the E§7 record is argued in E§7 itself, because it lies outside the
   382	frame domain. In summary:
   383	- the accepted values form a fixed-order regular language over alphabets that exclude the separators, so parsing
   384	  inverts encoding;
   385	  - this includes, since 2026-10-12, the mandatory `issuance-not-before` and `issuance-not-after` (the first was
   386	    optional until the Amendment 9 disposition), whose values are `YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ssZ`, one spelling per instant;
   387	  - `DNS_WINDOW` (not-after ≤ not-before) and `DNS_WINDOW_LENGTH` (not-after − not-before > 90 days) are checks on
   388	    parsed values and play no part in the split;
   389	- one RR with one character-string removes concatenation choices;
   390	- the owner name is compared as a sequence of labels, without regard to ASCII case.
