   394	## 4. Theorem (P8 for `tessera-v1-strict`)
   395	
   396	Let the accepted sets be as in §1.
   397	
   398	- **(i) Injectivity.** These maps are injective:
   399	  - `canonical = JCS` on V;
   400	  - `frame(t, ·)` on `Pay(t)`, and so `v ↦ frame(t, JCS(v))` on the schema-valid values;
   401	  - `A` on ordered (frame, signature) tuples `(F_kms, S_kms, F_gpg, S_gpg)` (Lemmas 7, 7a);
   402	  - `S` on snapshot bodies.
   403	
   404	  Each decoder is the exact inverse of its encoder on the accepted set: `Dec ∘ Enc = id` on accepted structures,
   405	  and `Enc ∘ Dec = id` on accepted bytes. *(Lemmas 3, 5, 7, 7a, 8.)*
   406	
   407	  So the map from an accepted receipt's structure `(M, C, S_kms, S_gpg)` to `(F_M, F_kms, F_gpg, A_sig)` is
   408	  injective outright:
   409	  - `F_M` determines `M`;
   410	  - `F_kms` determines `C`;
   411	  - `A_sig` determines `S_kms` and `S_gpg`, byte for byte.
   412	
   413	  The receipt is not determined by `(M, C)` alone (§1).
   414	
   415	- **(ii) Boundary-unambiguity.** In every accepted byte string, each field's extent is a function of the bytes
   416	  before it. The end of input is required to fall exactly where the last length prefix says. The JSON payload is
   417	  delimited by the frame's length, never by its own syntax. A signature's extent is fixed by its slot length, and
   418	  it must agree with the signature's own extent. *(Lemmas 5, 7, 7a, 8; Lemma 9 for the split of the OpenPGP hash
   419	  input into frame and trailer.)*
   420	
   421	- **(iii) Type and domain separation.** The accepted sets `Frm(attest)`, `Frm(manifest)`, `Anc` and `Snp` are
   422	  pairwise disjoint (Lemma 4). A decoder for one construction rejects every member of the others (`TAG_MISMATCH`).
   423	  The withdrawn frame-only anchor layout is rejected by every decoder (`TAG_UNKNOWN`; Lemma 7's corollary).
   424	
   425	  Inside `Frm(attest)`, the `kms` and `gpg` slot frames are disjoint, each frame's slot is fixed by its own bytes,
   426	  and an accepted receipt's two frames are distinct and byte-identical on `JCS(C)` (Lemma 6).
   427	
   428	  The DNS value and foreign formats are disjoint from all of these (Remark 10).
   429	
   430	- **(iv) Computational binding, under (H)** *(revised 2026-10-12)*. Each of the following binds only under SHA-256
   431	  collision resistance.
   432	  - A frame's signed bytes determine the manifest frame, the specification, the conformance vectors and the
   433	    preamble, through their SHA-256 values.
   434	  - The OTS-stamped digest `SHA256(A_sig)` determines the ordered tuple `((F_kms, S_kms), (F_gpg, S_gpg))`,
   435	    through Lemma 7: the frames **and the exact signature bytes**, in slot order.
   436	
   437	  So two different such objects bound by one accepted frame or anchor digest yield a SHA-256 collision. This is
   438	  the only place P8 for V1 rests on a cryptographic assumption.
   439	
   440	  **Four statements, kept apart** (finding 10). P8 proves (a) and (b). (c) and (d) are not P8's, and P8 does not
   441	  claim them.
   442	  - **(a) Codec injectivity** (unconditional). `A_sig` decodes to exactly one tuple and is re-encoded from it
   443	    (Lemma 7). A different signature byte string, even a valid one over the same frame, gives a different
   444	    preimage.
   445	  - **(b) Digest binding** (under (H)). A digest determines that tuple. A second tuple with the same digest is a
   446	    SHA-256 collision.
   447	  - **(c) Unforgeability** (P1 and P3, not P8). Whether `S` verifies over `F` under the slot key is a separate
   448	    check. It is made on the committed bytes (E§5.3). Whether a verifying `S` was produced by the key holder
   449	    rests on the signature scheme's security and on key custody.
   450	  - **(d) Temporal composition** (C5, not P8). Suppose valid OTS and Bitcoin evidence shows that

