     2	
     3	*Owner instance (Claude Opus 5.5), 2026-10-06. The full record is `RESULTS.md`; you do not need to read
     4	it. About fifteen minutes of reading. Nothing below is committed; your rulings come before the commit.*
     5	
     6	> **DEFERRED by the author, 2026-10-07 — no rulings requested now.** His words: *"I think ruling on this at
     7	> this time is premature. I do think we should preserve the results and proposed slicing method for future
     8	> use, but closing the capstone now is premature."* The capstone stays open until the throwaway prototype
     9	> has been used to look for missing implementation details, unsupported assumptions, mismatches with RFCs and
    10	> standards, and defects in the model's guarantees, with each finding sorted into *affects the capstone*
    11	> or *deferred to implementation*. This note is kept as written for when the rulings are due.
    12	
    13	## What the capstone establishes, in ordinary language
    14	
    15	**Claim.** The five verified pieces of Tessera's checking logic still hold when composed into one verifier.
    16	The five pieces are signer binding (S-P3), integrity (S-P1), signature-set completeness (S-P2), wrapper
    17	soundness (S-P7) and standing. Composition here means one intake, three wrapper layers, two signer slots
    18	and two issuers, against an attacker who controls the network and, depending on the case, holds some of
    19	the keys.
    20	
    21	**How we know the check isn't vacuous.** Every place where one piece relies on another (a "seam") has a
    22	deliberately broken companion model, and that companion must go red. A checker that never says red proves
    23	nothing.
    24	
    25	**Results, ladder 3** (the corrected model; 69 files ran; no verdict changed from ladder 2):
    26	- **Every correct-form query came out as registered (173 of 173), in all four compromise cases.** This is
    27	  the security result.
    28	- **56 broken companions came out as registered.** 10 have extra reds the registration did not predict.
    29	  They are registration defects, plus one genuine finding (G2: the standing verdict depends on authority
    30	  evidence in the same way acceptance does). None of them is a hole in the correct model.
    31	- **11 degraded-case companions could not finish in ProVerif.** These are the ones meant to show attacks
    32	  when the main signing key has leaked. Question 1 is about them.
    33	
    34	**Boundaries.** This is symbolic: perfect crypto, ideal parsing (P8 is separate), and a model of the
    35	verifier rather than the code. It cannot tell us whether the model matches the system we build. Only the
    36	conformance vectors against real code can (A1 §A1.7).
    37	
    38	## Decision 1: the eleven unfinished degraded companions
