 ## What is cut
 
 A10.4.1 and the rows that take effect only under option (e) with it: 25–32 and 37–39. These are the `service_keys`
    13	Use for a read-only review of Tessera ProVerif/TLA+ artifacts, S-P3, or `LEDGER.md` capstone claims. Do not use it to silently amend adopted artifacts, decide unresolved policy questions, or convert a scratch probe, companion, or viability run into discharge.
    14	
    15	## Inputs and context
    16	
    17	1. Establish the exact artifact, registered property/amendment, current adoption/status record, and requested decision.
    18	2. Read the relevant model and its header, predictions/results, theory library, and any routed review record. Amendments outrank trackers.
    19	3. Recheck live branch/status and preserve unrelated pre-existing work as user-owned.
    20	
    21	## Procedure
    22	
    23	1. Explain local terms before testing conclusions. Translate the review as: claim -> adversary -> load-bearing check -> assumptions -> non-claims -> validation evidence.
    24	2. State the model boundary precisely: which keys/channels are compromised, which signed bytes or frames are reused, and which attacks are outside scope.
    25	3. Run the registered command when practical, preserving tool version and exact result polarity. For S-P3 Q2 use:
    26	   ```sh
    27	   proverif -lib formal/suite/lib/tessera_theory.pvl formal/suite/s-p3/proverif/sp3_q2_degraded_compromised.pv
    28	   ```
    29	4. For `LEDGER.md`, read the full relevant contract and combined-consequences table, not a single section. Translate each producer/consumer relation into which component supplies information and which relies on it.
    30	5. For every claimed cross-model join, identify the registered producer query and a broken companion whose mutation makes the consuming query fail. If severing the producer does not affect the consumer's registered query, call the relation complementary or capstone-internal rather than cross-model.
    31	6. Check relation scope structurally. A key/signature transplant query cannot establish exact signing-to-presented-bytes binding: C-Q8 must inspect accepted `(key, signature, presented bytes)` triples. Require C-Q9 separately for possession over the accepted manifest, and an acceptance/execution identifier for C-Q1 completeness.
    32	7. For composition, ask whether all checks run on one acceptance path. A wrapper can attest that it saw a bundle, but cannot repair an original attestation that was already forgeable; separate envelope validity, standing, authorship, and adjudicator judgment.
    33	8. Separate registered evidence, scratch-probe evidence, header fidelity, property coverage, symbolic proof, concrete cryptographic/security claims, cross-formalism claims, and implementation conformance in the conclusion.
    34	
    35	## Efficiency plan
    36	
    37	- Start with the requested artifact plus its adjacent predictions/results and governing amendment; for a capstone review, also read the complete ledger contract and combined-consequences table.
    38	- Cache the claim/adversary/boundary table while comparing the model, header, and review record.
    39	- Stop and ask for a decision when an apparent inconsistency is a policy choice or the adoption state is unclear.
    40	
    41	## Pitfalls and fixes
    42	
    43	- Symptom: a bounded Q2 result is described as stolen-key or chain-takeover resistance. Cause: attacker scope expanded in prose. Fix: say it tests reattribution of existing honest signed bytes under explicitly modeled DSKS substitution; rotation/succession remains separate.
    44	- Symptom: a green companion/probe, bounded TLC run, or family-local query is treated as capstone discharge. Fix: preserve the distinction between scratch/negative-control evidence, registered coverage, cross-formalism proof, and final discharge.
    45	- Symptom: a ledger says “all findings accepted” or “exactly one red query.” Cause: scope/polarity was not checked. Fix: inspect the producer and consumer directly; record expected failed, retained, and witness sets when a mutation affects several queries.
    46	- Symptom: a copied check is called cross-model. Fix: sever the producer and require the registered consumer query to fail; otherwise label it complementary/capstone-internal.
    47	- Symptom: an accepted key/signature relation is used to claim exact presented-byte binding. Fix: inspect the complete accepted triple structurally and retain C-Q9's separate manifest-possession obligation.
    48	
    49	## Verification checklist
    50	
    51	- Confirm the command, model/library paths, and reported query results.
    52	- For capstone claims, confirm the producer query, consumer query, severing companion polarity, and whether evidence is registered rather than scratch-only.
    53	- Name explicit assumptions and non-claims, including concrete encoding/framing obligations when symbolic `fp` or `h` is used.
    54	- Confirm C-Q1 has an acceptance identifier, per-slot reports, same-acceptance completeness, strict/degraded distinction, evidence binding, possession, byte binding, type, and innermost-scope links.
    55	- Confirm conclusions do not move tracker/discharge status without recorded prerequisites, frozen predictions, joins, and agreement gates.
    47	- Amendment-10, `utc_assessment`, service-key-listing, attacker-selected keys, future adjudicator, trust provenance, release-key custody, R3-01, plain-language review, P8 vectors, differential testing
    48	
    49	## User preferences
    50	
    51	- When beginning Tessera work, the user explicitly welcomes read-only exploration and questions before edits -> start with repository orientation and questions, not edits or commits. [Task 1]
    52	- The user wants mechanical review separated from author decisions -> label facts, model/test evidence, owner recommendations, pending rulings, adopted changes, and unverified claims separately. [Task 1]
    53	- The user dislikes context-assuming “cold reads” -> assemble the current promise, assumptions, open decisions, and consequences in plain language instead of making them reconstruct amendment tables. [Task 1]
    54	- The user wants anti-sycophantic review and precise claims: distinguish “bytes/metadata were preserved or changed” from claims about truth, authorship, or meaning. [Task 1]
    55	
    56	## Reusable knowledge
    57	
    58	- Tessera’s central claim is binding exact presented bytes to signatures and a temporal anchor, not truth or submitter identity. Amend-don’t-rewrite applies: amendments outrank tracker summaries, and proposed/checked/discharged/owner-decided states must remain distinct. [Task 1]
    59	- Plain-language rendering and the prototype exposed gaps formal/document review missed: revocation lacked a source, relying parties could misread verdicts, byte layouts were underspecified, and Amendment 9 overclaimed Bitcoin header time. [Task 1]
    60	- Amendment 10’s service-key listing was cut in `docs/decisions/2026-10-19-listing-cut.md`; V1 therefore does not protect against new receipts under attacker-selected keys once both authority channels are captured. A receipt alone does not provide an authentic historical verifier/release package or trust provenance. [Task 1]
    61	- The retained HIGH R3-01 custody issue is that the reviewed build was not explicitly bound to the exact archive later signed/distributed. Green P8 vectors, end-to-end tests, independent-verifier tests, and a recorded 352/352 differential agreement are evidence of those checks, not a general service discharge. [Task 1]
    62	- The desired evidence model is preservation plus visible gaps: a chain cannot prove omitted history never existed, but expected anchors, links, or independently preserved records can make breaks visible. [Task 1]
    63	
    64	## Failures and how to do differently
    65	
    66	- Symptom: a local mechanism such as a service-key listing sounds complete. Cause: its future relying-party/bootstrap dependencies were not traced. Fix: follow every trust dependency through what a future adjudicator receives and how it is historically authenticated. [Task 1]
    67	- Symptom: “confirmation clean,” passing vectors, or differential agreement is treated as readiness. Fix: state the exact coverage; textual confirmation did not discharge implementation, publication, cryptographic, or human-test obligations. [Task 1]
    68	- Use construction, independent implementation, differential testing, mutation testing, and plain-language rendering early; the plain-language pilot exposed overclaims faster than repeated document reviews. [Task 1]
    69	
    70	# Task Group: Tessera MCP registration and tool allow-list repair

