   329	### 2d. Claim, adversary, boundary
   330	
   331	**Claim.** With two authority channels both required and one of them
   332	(DNS) fully compromised, no package is accepted except under an honest
   333	issuer's key, and then only over bytes that issuer signed. The first
   334	half is the first link (consumed: RESULTS ledger entry 2); the second
   335	is P1's binding half, the same as Q2's.
   336	
   337	**Adversary.** As Q2 with `skD` in place of `skS`; does not hold
   338	`skR`, `skI`, `skI2`.
   339	
   340	**Why (i) holds here and not in Q2.** Line 180. The honest channel
   341	signs only the two honest manifests, so the adversary cannot get a
   342	manifest naming its own key past the strict verifier; in Q2 there is
   343	no honest channel, and the adversary's own key gets through — which is
   344	why Q2 restricts the headline to honest keys and registers (ii) red.
   345	
   346	**Boundary.** As Q2, plus: `n = 2` channels, one leaked — not a
   347	general-`n` result (A1.3 item 6 "any proper subset", enumerated not
   348	generalized); the two-channel first link is *consumed* from the spike
   349	and S-P3 Q1, not re-established (no `AuthorityPublished*` conjunct is
   350	queried here — that is S-P3 Q1's query, `sp3_q1_*.pv:91–96`). And, as
   351	in §1d, three things cannot be posed in this model at all: signature-set
   352	operations (one signature slot, `ss` unused — S-P2's subject, review
   353	item 1); byte-encoding attacks (term equality for byte equality —
   354	P8's injectivity obligation and the library's `h`/`fp` idealization,
   355	Layer 2, review item 2); and verdicts, waivers or policy inputs (only
   356	`Accept` and non-acceptance — P4's partition, joined in prose, review
   357	item 3).
